.\" house.tmac -- the house style for groff documents in Notes.
.\"
.\" Loaded BY THE RENDERER, never by the document:
.\"   groff -Kutf8 -ww -dpaper=letter -P-pletter -F <font-dir> -P-e -ms \
.\"         -M <styles-dir> -m house -Tpdf - < doc > out.pdf
.\"
.\" Three things about that invocation are load-bearing:
.\"
.\" 1. -ms BEFORE -m house. The macro package sets LL, PO and friends when it
.\"    initialises; loading our file first meant ms silently overwrote the page
.\"    setup, and the measure came out at ms's default instead of ours.
.\" 2. The style is applied from the command line, so a document contains no
.\"    includes (.so/.mso), no font names, no page geometry and no raw escapes --
.\"    only the macros defined below. That is a security decision. Measured
.\"    2026-10-28 on groff 1.23: safer mode (on by default) refuses .sy/.pso/.pi/
.\"    .open/.opena with an explicit error, but it does NOT refuse .so, so
.\"    ".so /etc/hostname" reads the file and prints it into the output
.\"    (verified). Keeping includes out of documents removes that class, and the
.\"    renderer's allowlist refuses everything else.
.\" 3. -P-e forces every font to be embedded in the PDF, and -F points at the
.\"    directory scripts/groff-fonts.sh installs into. -F is only needed by the
.\"    dejavu set: the default set uses the faces groff mounts by itself.
.\"
.\" Keep this file pure ASCII. groff reads a macro file with the default input
.\" encoding, not -Kutf8, so a literal non-ASCII character in a COMMENT is an
.\" "invalid input character code" warning on every render. Use \[uXXXX] in the
.\" text and spell the character out in the prose.
.\"
.\" The document owns content; this file owns appearance. Retheme by editing here.
.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------- style sets
.\" A style set is the WHOLE appearance decision -- faces, type size, leading,
.\" measure, margins -- because the measure depends on the faces: Palatino at the
.\" same measure holds 76 characters where DejaVu Serif holds 62. Bundling them
.\" means a document cannot pick a font and keep a measure that no longer suits it.
.\"
.\" A DOCUMENT MAY CHOOSE A SET, and only from this list. It names a set, never a
.\" font: the request is looked up here, so a document still carries no font
.\" names, no sizes, no measures and no geometry, and an unknown name aborts the
.\" render (with "error:" on stderr and no PDF) instead of quietly producing
.\" something else. That is the whole reason this is safe.
.\"
.\" Measured 2026-10-28 on the pancake recipe, letter, one page either way:
.\"
.\"   palatino  12pt  5.2i  67 ch  731 KB  P052 / Nimbus Sans / Nimbus Mono
.\"   dejavu    11pt  5.5i  62 ch  2321 KB DejaVu Serif / Sans / Sans Mono
.\"
.\" palatino is the default. It is the URW base-35 family groff mounts itself, so
.\" nothing has to be installed, and gropdf embeds P052 anyway because it is not
.\" one of the PDF base-14 fonts. What it cannot set: 1/3, (1) and the smiley face
.\" are "special character not defined" and are dropped, so write 1/3 as 1/3.
.\" dejavu covers DejaVu's whole repertoire via \[uXXXX] and needs
.\" scripts/groff-fonts.sh installed (-F).
.\" A macro call as the body of an .ie cannot then be paired with an .el: measured
.\" 2026-10-28, that arrangement warns "unbalanced 'el' request", and the warning
.\" is reported at the CALL site, which makes it look like the caller's fault. So
.\" the dispatch below is plain .if lines plus a flag register.
.de @SET
.nr @KNOWN 1
.ds FONTBODY \\$1
.ds FONTHEAD \\$2
.ds FONTMONO \\$3
.ds SPECIAL \\$4
.nr PS \\$5
.nr VS \\$6
.nr LL \\$7
.nr PO \\$8
.fam \\*[FONTBODY]
.special \\*[SPECIAL]
..
.de STYLE
.nr @KNOWN 0
.if !\\n[.$] .ab error: .STYLE needs a set name -- the sets are palatino and dejavu
.if '\\$1'palatino' .@SET P H C S 12 14.5 5.2i 1.65i
.if '\\$1'dejavu' .@SET DS DV DM DVR 11 14.5 5.5i 1.5i
.if !\\n[@KNOWN] .ab error: unknown style '\\$1' -- the sets are palatino and dejavu
..
.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------- page and type
.\" Margins and spacing do not change between sets; the measure and type size do,
.\" and come from the set. Set by measurement, not intuition: the text extent in
.\" the PDF ends at exactly PO+LL, so nothing overflows the margin, and the filled
.\" lines stay inside the comfortable 45-75 characters. Re-measure after ANY
.\" change to a set.
.nr HM 0.9i           \" top margin
.nr FM 0.9i           \" bottom margin
.nr PI 0              \" no first-line indent: block paragraphs, spaced not indented
.nr PD 0.35v          \" paragraph and list-item spacing
.hy 1                 \" hyphenate (English patterns ship with groff)
.\" The default set; a document may override it with .STYLE <name>.
.STYLE palatino
.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------- fonts
.\" Names follow groff's family + style-letter convention, so R/B/I/BI work:
.\"
.\"   DS = DejaVu Serif      (body)     DSR DSB DSI DSBI
.\"   DV = DejaVu Sans       (headings) DVR DVB DVI DVBI
.\"   DM = DejaVu Sans Mono  (code)     DMR DMB DMI DMBI
.\"
.\" .special declares a font searched for a character the current one lacks, so
.\" \[u2460] (the circled digit one) renders even though DejaVu Serif has no
.\" circled digits; without it a missing character is a warning and a blank.
.fam \*[FONTBODY]
.special \*[SPECIAL]
.
.\" --------------------------------------------------------------- helpers
.\" One-line .if forms and helper macros keep this file free of conditional
.\" blocks -- easier to read, easier to diff.
.de @EM
.ft I
\\$*
.ft R
..
.\" A hairline rule the width of the measure, closing the title block. The width
.\" comes from \n[.l]u (the current line length in basic units) rather than a
.\" hard-coded value: a fixed 6.0i rule silently ran past the right margin.
.de @RULE
.sp 0.55v
\\l'\\n[.l]u'
.sp 0.55v
..
.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------- document vocabulary
.\" The document's title, set once at the top. The body lives in @TITLE so the
.\" two openers below cannot drift apart.
.\" Headings are set in the heading family against a body in another -- the
.\" contrast does the work a second size would otherwise have to do.
.de @TITLE
.in 0
.fam \\*[FONTHEAD]
.ps 16
.ft B
\\$1
.ft R
.ps
.fam \\*[FONTBODY]
.if \\n[.$]>1 .sp 0.25v
.if \\n[.$]>1 .@EM \\$2
.@RULE
..
.\" Two names, one title block, because a document's KIND is worth seeing in its
.\" own source: .RECIPE "Lemon drizzle cake" "serves 8" reads as a recipe,
.\" .DOC "Iterating over a vector" reads as everything else. Rendering is
.\" identical, so neither is a lesser choice and no document has to be re-typed.
.de DOC
.@TITLE \\$@
..
.de RECIPE
.@TITLE \\$@
..
.
.\" A section heading.
.\" .in 0 matters: .IP leaves an indent active, so without it a heading that
.\" follows a list is set one step in and the page reads as a staircase.
.\" .ne 3v keeps a heading with at least three lines of what it introduces,
.\" instead of stranding it at the foot of a page.
.de SECTION
.sp 1.0v
.in 0
.ne 3v
.fam \\*[FONTHEAD]
.ps 12
.ft B
\\$*
.ft R
.ps
.fam \\*[FONTBODY]
.sp 0.25v
..
.
.\" One ingredient line, hung as a bullet.  .ING "175 g unsalted butter"
.de ING
.IP \\(bu 3
\\$*
..
.
.\" One numbered step. Numbering is per document, counted in this register.
.nr STEPN 0
.de STEP
.nr STEPN +1
.IP "\\n[STEPN]." 4
\\$*
..
.
.\" A note, set off from the steps. The indent is absolute (4 ens, the same place
.\" .IP puts the step text) rather than relative, so the note lines up with the
.\" steps instead of stepping further in each time. Labelled and roman rather
.\" than all-italic: a note carries an instruction worth reading.
.de NOTE
.sp 0.45v
.in 4n
.ll -4n
\\fBNote.\\fR \\$*
.ll +4n
.in 0
.sp 0.45v
..
.
.\" Preformatted block: no filling, monospaced, aligned with the body text.
.\" For genuine two-column data a tbl table is the better answer -- run groff
.\" with -t and let the document carry .TS/.TE; this is for code-like blocks.
.de CODE
.sp 0.35v
.in 0
.nf
.fam \\*[FONTMONO]
.ft R
.ps 10
..
.de ENDCODE
.fam \\*[FONTBODY]
.ft R
.ps 11
.fi
.sp 0.35v
..
